WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.940
Speaker 1: All right. Let's start today, not&nbsp;
hyperbolically, but just in a way that aligns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:14.100
with the facts. Letting you know that the polling&nbsp;
for the 2022 midterms in both the House and Senate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:19.920
is looking a little bit worse for Democrats than&nbsp;
it was three weeks ago. There is a very clear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:28.260
although for now, not massive shift away from&nbsp;
Democrats towards Republicans in both the House&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:34.140
and Senate. And this just is another reminder&nbsp;
that we all have to vote and we have to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:40.260
nothing for granted. Let's start first with the&nbsp;
House of Representatives. Republicans have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:47.760
favored to win the House and they remain favored&nbsp;
to win the House. But the the rate the probability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:54.720
of that event taking place has increased. And if&nbsp;
we look at the FiveThirtyEight forecast of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:01.920
House of Representatives, one of the things that&nbsp;
you see is that over time, the Democratic chances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:08.820
of controlling the House surged from about&nbsp;
June through late September and then started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:16.500
to decline. Now it is not a massive change, but&nbsp;
there is this inflection point in late September,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:22.560
early October, wherein Republican odds of&nbsp;
controlling the House, taking control of the House&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:29.820
have increased. What do we do about it? Well, we&nbsp;
all have to vote. That doesn't change. But this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:35.580
is starting to look like a trend. We then look&nbsp;
at the Senate. Democrats have been for a while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:43.860
and remain favored to control the Senate after&nbsp;
the midterms to retain control. But again, if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:49.740
you look at how those odds have changed, you see&nbsp;
an even more notable inflection point. Again, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:56.940
was right around, I don't know, mid-September. And&nbsp;
since mid-September, Democratic odds have declined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:04.980
and Republican odds have increased. This doesn't&nbsp;
change that. The most likely outcome, the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:12.240
likely single outcome is that Democrats retain&nbsp;
control of the Senate and Democrats lose control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:17.820
of the House. That's been the most statistically&nbsp;
likely outcome for a while now, but it's looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:25.380
a little bit less good in the in the Senate and&nbsp;
even worse than it was in the House. So what is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:29.700
the takeaway? Later on in the show, we're going to&nbsp;
be looking at more debates from yesterday. We're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:35.580
going to be looking at other polling and specific&nbsp;
races. The takeaway is simply every single state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:42.900
has to be fought for and whether it's looking&nbsp;
like an easy Democratic win. So, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:47.220
there is a key race in Michigan. The governor&nbsp;
of Michigan, Gretchen Whitmer, the incumbent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:53.040
trying to beat off a challenge from Tudor Dixon.&nbsp;
And Whitmer is up by somewhere between eight and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:57.840
11 points. It's looking pretty safe. But that does&nbsp;
that does that mean our Michigander viewers stay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:04.440
home? I certainly hope not. Then you have very,&nbsp;
very tight races like, for example, in Arizona,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:11.400
the Arizona gubernatorial where Carrie Lake, the&nbsp;
deranged big lie conspiracy theorist Republican,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:19.440
is about half a point to one point ahead of Katie&nbsp;
Hobbs. That's a disaster. Do we stay home there?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:23.460
No, actually, Arizona is one of the main places,&nbsp;
as I talked about yesterday, where we have to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:27.600
sure everybody's voting. And then there are&nbsp;
other races that are looking more in favor of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:31.800
Republicans who an example would be tonight,&nbsp;
there's a debate in this race. Marco Rubio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:37.020
the incumbent Republican senator of Florida, he is&nbsp;
facing a challenge from Democratic Congresswoman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:42.720
Val Demings. He's up like five or so. Do we give&nbsp;
that one up? Know whether the Democrat is up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:48.480
the Republican is up or it's a tight race? You're&nbsp;
allowed to vote. So take advantage and make sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:53.160
that you do vote. And we'll look in more detail&nbsp;
at some of these other races a little bit later.

